## Context

Author, 2026-10-30: test-cache management is expert knowledge the test doctor
applies on demand, from evidence on repeated runs, not a wording pin in every
agent's brief. The toolkit already lists "a rule telling every agent to use a
cache" under Not remedies; what it lacks is the positive side: the syndrome,
the remedy, and a probe that sees it.

Prototype on the harness's own traces (28 days, padme, 2026-10-30): 4477
foreground gate runs, 1326 with a readable pytest summary line; 84 red runs
followed by a rerun of the same gate in the same session, 101 min of rerun
wait; median red 1-2 tests of ~2100, median rerun 75 s. 52 of the reds went
green with no Edit/Write call between, a flip-candidate count the probe must
qualify (this repo also edits through Bash and git).

Pytest's cache (`.pytest_cache/v/cache/{lastfailed,nodeids,stepwise}`) holds
test ids only, is worktree-local and cold at creation, and goes stale silently:
the primary checkout's `lastfailed` on 2026-10-30 named four tests, none of
which still exists on main.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `scripts/gate_traces.py reruns` reports, per gate, red-to-rerun chains, rerun wait, median red and suite size, a derived upper bound on rerun time spent on previously passing tests, and red-to-green flips with no recognised edit between; outcome-read coverage printed so "none found" differs from "could not read"
- [ ] Tests: a positive control (a red run then a full rerun is a chain), a flip, an edited fix, an unreadable outcome
- [ ] test-doctor examination names the probe; syndromes gain the fix-loop rerun syndrome and use flips as flake evidence; toolkit gains the runner's last-failed cache as a remedy with prerequisites, staleness and its bounds (never a gate, never a blanket rule)
- [ ] `make check` passes
